== 3.1 Three Muscle Types

*Skeletal muscle* attaches to the skeleton through tendons and is involved in movement and posture. It is generally under voluntary control, although reflexes and automatic adjustments can also involve skeletal muscles. *Cardiac muscle* forms the muscular wall of the heart. *Smooth muscle* occurs in walls of structures such as the digestive tract and blood vessels. Cardiac and smooth muscle activity is generally involuntary.

Muscle fibres can generate force when activated. Force production depends on several factors, including the task, muscle condition and nervous control. A contraction does not always mean a muscle becomes shorter: in an *isometric* action the muscle produces force without an appreciable change in overall muscle length, while joint movement is limited or absent.

#rule-box("Pull, not push")[
  A skeletal muscle pulls through a tendon on a bone. Muscles can work in pairs or groups to control movement in different directions; a single muscle does not simply push the bone back to its starting position.
]

== 3.2 Antagonistic Action at the Elbow

During a simple elbow-flexion example, the *biceps brachii* contributes to bending the elbow while the *triceps brachii* acts in the opposite direction. During extension, the triceps contributes to straightening. Other muscles also contribute, and both muscles can be active together for control and stability; the familiar pair is a simplified teaching model.

