== 3.3 The Nervous System and Movement

The *central nervous system* consists of the brain and spinal cord. Nerves connect the central nervous system with other parts of the body. Sensory information can inform a response; motor pathways carry signals that contribute to muscle activation. Movement depends on coordination among the nervous system, muscles, joints and the environment.

A *reflex* is a relatively rapid response to a stimulus through a neural pathway. A simplified reflex arc includes a receptor, sensory pathway, central connection and motor pathway to an effector. Actual pathways differ; a reflex is not proof that the brain has no role in processing the event.

#concept-box("Trace the basic path")[
  A receptor detects a stimulus; sensory information travels to the nervous system; a motor pathway activates an effector such as a muscle. The diagram is a model, not a complete map of all neural connections.
]

== 3.4 Movement in Practice

Warm-up, skill practice, rest and gradual progression can support a training session, but they do not eliminate every risk. Fatigue can affect performance and coordination. Clear instructions and suitable rest intervals can help learners maintain movement quality; the response to fatigue still differs between individuals. The appropriate amount of activity depends on the person and context; stop and seek appropriate help for concerning symptoms or injury rather than treating pain as a required sign of progress.

== 3.5 Four Worked Examples
#worked-example(1, "The heart", tier: "FOUNDATION",
  [The muscle forming the heart wall is \
A. cardiac muscle \
B. a tendon \
C. a ligament \
D. a bone],
  [Cardiac muscle forms the heart's muscular wall. The answer is *A*.])
#worked-example(2, "An elbow movement", tier: "UTME STANDARD",
  [The triceps brachii contributes principally to \
A. elbow flexion only \
B. elbow extension \
C. protecting the skull \
D. filtering blood],
  [The triceps contributes to straightening the elbow, or extension. The answer is *B*.])
#worked-example(3, "Central control", tier: "UTME STANDARD",
  [The central nervous system consists of the \
A. heart and lungs \
B. tendons and ligaments \
C. brain and spinal cord \
D. femur and humerus],
  [The brain and spinal cord form the central nervous system. The answer is *C*.])
#worked-example(4, "Force without obvious movement", tier: "JAMB CHALLENGE",
  [A person holds a position while producing muscle force without appreciable change in muscle length. This is an \
A. open fracture \
B. involuntary heartbeat \
C. bone movement without muscle \
D. isometric action],
  [An isometric action produces force without an appreciable change in overall muscle length. The answer is *D*.])

#trap-box("Avoid the oversimplification")[
  The biceps and triceps are a useful teaching pair, not the only muscles involved. A skeletal muscle can be involved in reflex or automatic control as well as voluntary movement.
]

